DAVID TU: Hey, everyone.

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:02.520
David Tu here.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.050
And I'm excited to
be back to share

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:05.940
some updates on
our new JA launches

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.220
for load balancing and
service networking.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:08.925
Stick around.

00:00:08.925 --> 00:00:12.012
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.260
We'll kick off this
video discussing

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.710
new features available for both
internal and external HTTPS

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.160
load balancer.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:24.210
This feature enables you
to test URL maps that

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.220
has already been deployed.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.190
As a refresher, a URL map
is a configuration resource

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:32.850
that routes requests to backend
services or backend buckets.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:37.230
For example, example.com/video
can go to one backend service.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:40.710
Example.com/audio can go to
a different backend service.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:45.000
And example.com/images can go to
a Cloud Storage backend bucket.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.580
Not only can you use URL
maps to route traffic

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.870
based on the path, but you can
also route based on the host.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.390
This flexibility is crucial
for traffic engineering,

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:57.180
but could get overwhelming
in validations.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.460
With this launch, you
can easily configure

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.100
tests to verify whether
a specific URL are

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.319
hitting the expected backend.

00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:07.170
When running a test, we
will identify the backend

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.410
you expect versus the backend
that traffic will actually

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:11.850
be routed to.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.890
Moving on to the internal
network load balancer,

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.720
we have several
updates to share.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.370
We'll start off
with an enhancement

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.550
when using the internal network
load balancer as a next hop

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:21.660
for routing.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.330
For context, you can
create a static route,

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:27.420
which sends traffic to
an internal network load

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.370
balancer as the next hop.

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:32.580
In this example, we're using the
internal network load balancer

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.160
to balance traffic between
multiple NAT gateways

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.450
that route traffic
to the internet.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:40.320
This was available
only for TCP and UDP.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.930
With this launch, we now
support all protocols,

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:45.630
which allows for more
functionality and easier

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.420
troubleshooting.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:48.830
Next feature is
something crucial

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:51.500
when deploying stateful virtual
appliances in Google Cloud,

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.150
symmetric hashing.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:55.040
Prior to this launch,
whenever deploying

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.950
stateful virtual appliances
in a highly available manner,

00:01:57.950 --> 00:01:59.870
it would require
source NAT to ensure

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:02.510
the return traffic would
be properly routed back

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:03.860
to the same appliance.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.600
With the launch of
symmetric hashing,

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.900
we simplified operations for
stateful virtual appliances

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:13.160
by guaranteed traffic of the
same flow gets the same hash.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.140
In other words, the
hash doesn't change

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:18.170
when the source IP and port is
swapped with the destination

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:19.250
IP and port.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.050
This ensures the
return traffic hits

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:23.060
a corresponding
virtual appliance

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.820
and removes the
requirement for source NAT.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:28.880
Lastly, for the internal
network load balancer,

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.970
we've added some functionality
for UDP protocol session

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:33.020
affinity.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:35.270
Prior to this launch,
only TCP sessions

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:38.580
supported two-tuple and
three-tuple session affinity.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.400
Now the internal
network load balancer

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.790
can ensure UDP connections from
the same source and destination

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:47.480
IP or source and destination
IP and protocol go

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.460
to the same backend instance.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:52.730
Saving what I'm most excited to
share for last, Private Service

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:53.570
Connect.

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:55.940
Private Service Connect
enables private consumption

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:58.160
of services across
VPCs belonging

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.540
to different groups, teams,
projects, or organizations.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:06.170
It is simple, scalable, and
secure, since the traffic never

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:07.790
goes over the internet.

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:10.460
You may recall my previous
episode covered Private Service

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:12.380
Connect for Google APIs.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:14.900
That update allowed users
to create custom endpoints

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.490
for Google APIs.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:19.100
This update is to use Private
Service Connect to publish

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:20.600
and consume services.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.090
Private Service Connect
solves many of the caveats

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:26.930
that current private VPC-to-VPC
solutions, like VPC peering,

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:28.370
introduce.

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:32.030
With Private Service Connect,
IP addressing and IP usage

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:33.980
is drastically simplified.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.320
Consumers and producers
don't need to share quotas

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.840
and limits, and it allows
for overlapping addresses

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:40.370
between the VPCs.

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:42.110
Private Service
Connect also allows

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:44.010
for multitenant services.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:46.070
This means different
consumers can connect

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:47.990
to a multitenant service.

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:49.970
With Private Service
Connect, producers

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:53.420
can significantly scale in
a private and secure manner.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:55.910
This gives flexibility
to a SaaS provider,

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:59.690
as it enables easy and
secure private connectivity.

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:01.160
And that's a wrap
for this episode

